<title>FRANCE: Thomson-CSF shares bounce back.</title>
<headline>Thomson-CSF shares bounce back.</headline>
<dateline>PARIS 1997-05-27</dateline>
<text>
<p>Shares in defence electronics group Thomson-CSF bounced back on Tuesday after a decline on Monday on doubts whether its privatisation would be continued if the left won the French legislative election.</p>
<p>At 0812 GMT, Thomson-CSF was 0.23 percent higher at 171.60. The share fell 9.60 percent on Monday.  </p>
<p>&quot;There is a bit of a play going on. Some people who sold yesterday are buying back today,&quot; a dealer said.</p>
<p>&quot;There were fears the privatisation programme would be stopped but there is a growing realisation a Socialist-led government will also have to sell shares. The amounts involved are considerable and they will need it for their budget,&quot; the trader added.</p>
<p>The current government said it would decide before the end of June whether to sell a 58-percent stake in Thomson-CSF to a team of Alcatel-Alsthom and private Dassault Industrie or to Lagardere Groupe.</p>
<p>Other privatisation-related stocks that fell on Monday were computer maker Cie des Machines Bull, up 0.12 percent at 42.95 francs, and bank Credit Lyonnais, down 1.27 percent at 210 francs.</p>
<p>Aviation Dassault, suspended limit down on Monday, was twice suspended on Tuesday morning -- once limit-down and once limit-up. It is set to requote at 0900 GMT.</p>
